<div id=3D"S1.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p" data-imt-p=3D"1">A recent advancement&nbs=
p;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib92" title=
=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> in 3D representation learning has ma=
de progress in overcoming the data scale limitation in point cloud processi=
ng by introducing a synergistic training approach spanning multiple 3D data=
sets. Coupled with this strategy, the efficient convolutional backbone&nbsp=
;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib13" title=3D=
"" class=3D"ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> has effectively bridged the accuracy ga=
p commonly associated with point cloud transformers&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx=
_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_im=
mersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib90" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref=
">90</a>, <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate=
_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib40" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.=
 However, point cloud transformers themselves have not yet fully benefited =
from this privilege of scale due to their efficiency gap compared to sparse=
 convolution. This discovery shapes the initial motivation for our work: <s=
pan id=3D"S1.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">to re-weigh the des=
ign choices in point transformers, with the lens of the scaling principle</=
span>. We posit that model performance is more significantly influenced by =
