lass=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Serialization-based method.</span>
Recent works&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"h=
ttps://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bi=
b.bib83" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">83</a>, <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib51" title=3D=
"" class=3D"ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_=
immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib7" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_re=
f">7</a>]</cite> have introduced approaches diverging from the traditional =
paradigms of point cloud processing, which we categorized as serialization-=
based. These methods structure point clouds by sorting them according to sp=
ecific patterns, transforming unstructured, irregular point clouds into man=
ageable sequences while preserving certain spatial proximity. OctFormer&nbs=
p;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib83" title=
=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> inherits order during octreelization=
, akin to z-order, offering scalability but still constrained by the octree=
 structure itself. FlatFormer&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_ci=
te">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto=
_translate=3D1#bib.bib51" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, on t=
he other hand, employs a window-based sorting strategy for grouping point p=
illars, akin to window partitioning. However, this design lacks scalability=
 in the receptive field and is more suited to pillar-based 3D object detect=
ors. These pioneering works mark the inception of serialization-based metho=
ds. Our PTv3 builds on this foundation, defining and exploring the full pot=
ential of point cloud serialization.<font class=3D"notranslate immersive-tr=
