<p id=3D"A2.SS2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p" data-imt-p=3D"1">Previous point trans=
formers use a traditional block structure that sequentially applies an oper=
ator, a normalization layer, and an activation function. While effective, t=
his approach can sometimes complicate training deeper models due to issues =
like vanishing gradients or the need for careful initialization and learnin=
g rate adjustments&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a hre=
f=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=
=3D1#bib.bib95" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite>. Consequently, =
we explored adopting a more modern block structure, such as pre-norm and po=
st-norm. The pre-norm structure, where a normalization layer precedes the o=
perator, can stabilize training by ensuring normalized inputs for each laye=
r&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxi=
v.org/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib12" ti=
tle=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In contrast, the post-norm struc=
ture places a normalization layer right after the operator, potentially lea=
ding to faster convergence but with less stability&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_=
cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_imm=
ersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#bib.bib80" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref"=
>80</a>]</cite>. Our experimental results (see Tab.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_immersive_translate_auto_translate=3D1#A1.T18" =
title=3D"Table 18 =E2=80=A3 A.1 Training Settings =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Impl=
ementation Details =E2=80=A3 Point Transformer V3: Simpler, Faster, Stronge=
r" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) in=
dicate that the pre-norm structure is more suitable for our PTv3, aligning =
with findings in recent transformer-based models&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_ci=
te ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10035?_immer=
